# React Front-end Performance

**Golden rule:** `memo` / `useMemo` / `useCallback` are the **last resortd**, not the first. Fix state architecture first (where it lives, who subscribes); memoization only patches symptoms.

**Measure first:** Before touching code, you need data. Optimizing without measuring is the classic trap.

## Diagnostic flow

| Symptom | Skill |
|---------|-------|
| Long blank screen on open / poor LCP | `fe-perf-initial-load-bundle` |
| UI frozen on typing, scroll, or click / poor INP | `fe-perf-runtime-rerenders` |
| Duplicate fetches, huge datasets, WebSockets | `fe-perf-data-network` |
| No baseline or can't reproduce the issue | `fe-perf-measure-first` |

## Core Web Vitals (quick reference)

| Metric | Good | Poor |
|--------|------|------|
| LCP | < 2.5s | > 4s |
| INP | < 200ms | > 500ms |
| CLS | < 0.1 | > 0.25 |

## Priority order when generating code

1. State architecture (placement, granular selectors)
2. Long list virtualization
3. Debounce / deferred updates on inputs
4. Code splitting by route and feature
5. TanStack Query for server state
6. `memo` / `useMemo` / `useCallback` — only where the Profiler justifies it
